insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2952, $q$Faithful Without the Spotlight$q$, $q$faithful-without-the-spotlight$q$, replace($pt$We often measure a life by how much material it leaves behind. We count years, accomplishments, followers, and recorded words. Abel’s story offers almost none of that, yet the Bible refuses to call his life insignificant. View the map for this story.[[newline]][[newline]]Abel (AY-buhl) was the second son of Adam and Eve and the younger brother of Cain. Genesis describes him simply as a keeper of flocks. Cain worked the soil (Genesis 4:1–2).[[newline]][[newline]]In time, both brothers brought offerings to God. Cain brought some of the fruits of the soil. Abel brought fat portions from some of the firstborn of his flock. God looked with favor on Abel and his offering, but not on Cain and his offering (Genesis 4:3–5).[[newline]][[newline]]Genesis does not give a complete explanation of that difference. Later Scripture says Abel offered by faith and that his offering was better than Cain’s (Hebrews 11:4). The story does not authorize us to invent details it does not supply.[[newline]][[newline]]Cain became angry. God warned him that sin was crouching at his door and that he must rule over it. Cain later took Abel into a field and killed him (Genesis 4:5–8).[[newline]][[newline]]Abel speaks no recorded words. He does not defend himself, build a movement, or get the chance to explain his offering. God still hears what violence tried to silence: “Your brother’s blood cries out to me from the ground” (Genesis 4:10, NIV).[[newline]][[newline]]That line matters to anyone whose good work feels unseen. Recognition is not what gives faithfulness its value. A caregiver may repeat the same quiet tasks each day. An employee may tell the truth in a room that rewards convenience. A friend may keep showing up without ever becoming central to the story.[[newline]][[newline]]Abel’s death also keeps us from romanticizing hidden service. Being unnoticed by people can leave someone vulnerable. Faithfulness deserves protection, and violence deserves to be named rather than covered with spiritual language.[[newline]][[newline]]Jesus later referred to “righteous Abel” when speaking about innocent blood (Matthew 23:35). Hebrews says Abel still speaks, even though he is dead (Hebrews 11:4).[[newline]][[newline]]His life was brief, but its meaning was not. I do not need a large platform before honesty, worship, or careful work matters. I can bring the best I have to the work in front of me.[[newline]][[newline]]The spotlight may never arrive. Faithfulness is still visible where it counts, and its quiet witness can outlive us.$pt$, '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-05-02T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 21, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2950, $q$The Help Pride Almost Missed$q$, $q$the-help-pride-almost-missed$q$, replace($pt$Help is easy to miss when we have already decided what it should look like. We may expect an expert, a dramatic plan, or an answer that confirms our importance. Then the useful advice arrives through an ordinary person and sounds almost too simple.[[newline]][[newline]]Naaman (NAY-uh-muhn) commanded the army of Aram, a kingdom centered around Damascus in modern Syria. Second Kings calls him a great man and a valiant soldier. It also says he had a serious skin disease (2 Kings 5:1). View the map for this story.[[newline]][[newline]]The first person to point him toward help was an unnamed Israelite girl captured in an Aramean raid. She served Naaman’s wife and said the prophet in Samaria could heal him (2 Kings 5:2–3).[[newline]][[newline]]Naaman went to Israel with horses, chariots, expensive gifts, and a letter from his king. But the prophet Elisha did not come out to greet him. A messenger told Naaman to wash seven times in the Jordan River (2 Kings 5:4–10).[[newline]][[newline]]Naaman became angry. He had imagined Elisha standing before him, calling on God, and waving a hand over the diseased skin. He also considered the rivers of Damascus better than the Jordan. He turned away in a rage (2 Kings 5:11–12).[[newline]][[newline]]Then servants helped him again. They respectfully asked whether he would have done some difficult thing if the prophet had required it. Why not do the simple thing he had been told? Naaman listened, dipped himself seven times in the Jordan, and was healed (2 Kings 5:13–14).[[newline]][[newline]]The powerful commander received his direction from a captive girl, a prophet’s messenger, and his own servants. Every one of them stood below him in the social order. Any one of them could have been ignored.[[newline]][[newline]]I can recognize that temptation. I may ask for feedback while quietly ranking the people allowed to give it. I may dismiss a small solution because it does not feel equal to the size of my problem.[[newline]][[newline]]Listening does not mean accepting every suggestion. It means judging the message on its truth and usefulness, not merely on the status of the person speaking.[[newline]][[newline]]Naaman’s rank could organize an army, but it could not heal him. He needed enough humility to follow a simple instruction and enough wisdom to let servants change his mind.[[newline]][[newline]]The help we need may already be in the room. Pride asks whether the voice is impressive. Humility asks whether it is telling the truth.$pt$, '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-04-25T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 22, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2948, $q$Leadership Still Needs Correction$q$, $q$leadership-still-needs-correction$q$, replace($pt$Years of faithful work can quietly turn into a shield. We remember the risks we took, the people we helped, and the times we were right. Then correction feels less like guidance and more like disrespect.[[newline]][[newline]]Miriam (MIHR-ih-uhm) had a record worth honoring. As a girl in Egypt, she watched over her baby brother Moses after their mother placed him in a basket among the reeds of the Nile. When Pharaoh’s daughter found him, Miriam offered to find a Hebrew woman to nurse him, helping return Moses to his own mother for a time (Exodus 2:1–10). View the map for this story.[[newline]][[newline]]Years later, after Israel escaped Egypt and crossed the sea, Miriam was called a prophet. She took a tambourine, led the women in music and dancing, and sang about God’s victory (Exodus 15:20–21).[[newline]][[newline]]She was not a background figure. The prophet Micah later named Miriam alongside Moses and Aaron as leaders God sent before Israel (Micah 6:4).[[newline]][[newline]]Then Numbers records a harder moment. Miriam and Aaron spoke against Moses because of his Cushite wife. They also asked whether God had spoken only through Moses and not through them (Numbers 12:1–2).[[newline]][[newline]]The text does not dismiss their leadership history. It does not let that history settle the dispute either. God confronted them, defended Moses’s particular calling, and Miriam was struck with a serious skin disease. Aaron admitted their wrongdoing, and Moses prayed for her healing (Numbers 12:4–13).[[newline]][[newline]]The whole community waited seven days until Miriam could return to the camp (Numbers 12:14–15). The leader who had once helped protect Moses now received protection, prayer, and time to recover.[[newline]][[newline]]I find both warning and mercy there. A strong contribution does not make my next judgment correct. If I have served for years, I may have more experience—but I also have more reason to listen carefully when trusted people challenge me.[[newline]][[newline]]Correction does not erase what came before. Miriam still helped save Moses. She still led the song. Scripture still remembers her as one of Israel’s leaders.[[newline]][[newline]]That gives me a better way to receive hard truth. I do not have to defend my entire life when one decision is questioned. I can examine the decision, own what needs owning, and accept help.[[newline]][[newline]]A faithful past is a gift. It is not immunity. Mature leadership keeps enough humility to be corrected and enough hope to return with grace.$pt$, '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-04-18T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 23, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2946, $q$The Burden You Did Not Choose$q$, $q$the-burden-you-did-not-choose$q$, replace($pt$Some responsibilities arrive without asking whether we are ready. A family member becomes ill. A colleague leaves. A stranger needs help at the worst possible time. We did not choose the burden, but we still have to decide how we will carry it.[[newline]][[newline]]Simon of Cyrene came into Jesus’s story that way. Cyrene (sai-REE-neh) was a North African city near modern Shahhat, Libya, more than eight hundred miles west of Jerusalem. Mark identifies Simon as the father of Alexander and Rufus, but tells us nothing about why he was in Jerusalem that day (Mark 15:21). View the map for this story.[[newline]][[newline]]Jesus had been condemned to crucifixion. Roman soldiers led him out of the city toward Golgotha. Along the way, they forced Simon, who was passing by on his way in from the country, to carry Jesus’s cross (Mark 15:20–22).[[newline]][[newline]]Simon did not volunteer. The text does not tell us what he felt, what he believed, or whether he understood who Jesus was. It gives us one observable fact: imperial power pulled a bystander from the road and placed the wooden burden on him.[[newline]][[newline]]That matters because we sometimes make service sound like a moment of perfect willingness. Simon’s story is not that neat. He was interrupted, compelled, and placed inside someone else’s suffering.[[newline]][[newline]]Luke says the soldiers put the cross on Simon and made him carry it behind Jesus (Luke 23:26). The wording creates a picture Jesus had already used for discipleship: “Whoever wants to be my disciple must deny themselves and take up their cross daily and follow me” (Luke 9:23, NIV). Luke does not explain the connection, but the image is hard to miss.[[newline]][[newline]]Most of our unwanted burdens are not crosses, and another person’s emergency does not automatically become our permanent assignment. Limits still matter. Help can include calling someone better equipped, sharing the load, or saying what we cannot safely do.[[newline]][[newline]]But I do not want surprise to become my excuse for indifference. I can pause long enough to ask what care is possible now. Perhaps I can make the call, carry the bag, stay for the appointment, or take one task from someone who is exhausted.[[newline]][[newline]]Simon’s part in the story is brief, but it is not invisible. A burden he did not choose became an act remembered in three Gospels. Readiness did not come first. The next faithful step did.$pt$, '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-04-11T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 24, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2943, $q$Power Cannot Outsource Responsibility$q$, $q$power-cannot-outsource-responsibility$q$, replace(replace($pt$Avoiding a decision can feel safer than making the wrong one. But when I have the power to protect someone, silence is still a choice. The consequences do not disappear because I kept my hands clean.[[newline]][[newline]]Ahab (AY-hab) was king of Israel in the ninth century BC. He ruled from Samaria, in the hill country of today’s West Bank, and kept a royal residence at Jezreel, about twenty miles north. His wife, Queen Jezebel, strongly promoted the worship of Baal, while the prophet Elijah repeatedly called the nation back to God (1 Kings 16:29–33[[semicolon]] 18:17–21). View the map for this story.[[newline]][[newline]]One story makes Ahab’s leadership painfully clear. A man named Naboth owned a vineyard beside Ahab’s palace at Jezreel. Ahab wanted it for a vegetable garden. Naboth refused because the land was his family inheritance (1 Kings 21:1–3).[[newline]][[newline]]Ahab went home angry, lay on his bed, turned his face to the wall, and refused to eat. When Jezebel asked what was wrong, he told her. She promised to get the vineyard for him (1 Kings 21:4–7).[[newline]][[newline]]She used Ahab’s royal authority to send letters in his name. The letters arranged false charges against Naboth. He was taken outside the city and killed. Then Jezebel told Ahab the vineyard was available, and he went down to claim it (1 Kings 21:8–16).[[newline]][[newline]]The text does not say Ahab wrote the letters or threw the stones. It also does not let him hide behind that fact. Elijah met him in the vineyard and announced judgment because Ahab had taken possession after Naboth’s murder (1 Kings 21:17–24).[[newline]][[newline]]That lands close to ordinary life. A supervisor may not invent the cruel joke, falsify the report, or exclude the difficult colleague. But if the supervisor knows, has authority, and chooses comfort over action, that quiet choice shapes the workplace.[[newline]][[newline]]I need to ask more than, “Did I cause this?” I also need to ask, “What could I have stopped?” Power includes responsibility for what I permit, reward, and quietly accept.[[newline]][[newline]]Ahab later humbled himself when he heard Elijah’s judgment, and God noticed that response (1 Kings 21:27–29). Even this story leaves room for a change in direction.[[newline]][[newline]]Leadership does not require controlling everything. It does require owning the choices that belong to me. If my name, position, or silence gives harm room to grow, I cannot outsource the responsibility for what follows.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-04-04T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 25, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1792, $q$The Watchman Who Kept Showing Up$q$, $q$when-nobody-wants-to-hear-it-2$q$, replace(replace($pt$Waiting can make a person feel forgotten. Days keep moving while the answer, change, or relief we hoped for remains out of reach. The danger is not only disappointment[[semicolon]] it is letting delay convince us that nothing faithful can happen now.[[newline]][[newline]]Ezekiel’s calling teaches that leadership is not validated only by immediate response. God asks his servants to bear witness faithfully.[[newline]][[newline]]Ezekiel was a priest among the Judean exiles in Babylon. By the Chebar canal, in the region of modern Iraq, he received visions and a prophetic commission after Jerusalem’s first deportation (Ezekiel 1–3).[[newline]][[newline]]God warned Ezekiel that his audience was rebellious and hard-hearted. The warning did not cancel the assignment: “Speak my words to them, whether they listen or fail to listen” (Ezekiel 2:7).[[newline]][[newline]]His ministry included symbolic actions, difficult messages of judgment, and visions that revealed both God’s holiness and Judah’s corruption. These signs were not theatrics for attention. They made visible the seriousness of covenant unfaithfulness and the certainty of coming judgment.[[newline]][[newline]]God called Ezekiel a watchman (Ezekiel 3:17[[semicolon]] 33:7). A watchman cannot force people to respond to danger. His responsibility is to see clearly, sound the warning, and remain at his post. That distinction protects leaders from both despair and manipulation.[[newline]][[newline]]Ezekiel did not end with judgment. He proclaimed God’s promise of a new heart and a new spirit (Ezekiel 36:26), saw dry bones receive life by God’s breath (Ezekiel 37:1–14), and witnessed the glory of the Lord return (Ezekiel 43:1–5). Restoration belonged to God.[[newline]][[newline]]The leadership application is not that outcomes are unimportant. It is that obedience cannot be reduced to applause, attendance, or quick agreement. We must speak truth with humility, remain accountable to God, and trust him to bring life where human effort cannot.[[newline]][[newline]]A long search for work, an unresolved illness, or a strained relationship can narrow our attention to what has not happened. We still may be able to keep one promise, encourage one person, ask for help, or notice one good thing without pretending the pain is gone.[[newline]][[newline]]One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.[[newline]][[newline]]Waiting is not proof that a life has stopped mattering. I want to stay honest about what hurts while remaining available to the good I can receive and give today.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-03-28T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 26, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1934, $q$The Message No One Wanted to Hear$q$, $q$the-message-no-one-wanted-to-hear$q$, replace(replace($pt$Truth can become expensive when a lie is already protecting someone's comfort, reputation, or plan. The longer silence lasts, the easier it is to call avoidance wisdom. Yet hidden trouble usually grows while everyone pretends not to see it.[[newline]][[newline]]Spiritual decline rarely begins with a dramatic rejection of God. More often, it begins when worship becomes convenient and obedience becomes selective.[[newline]][[newline]]Malachi ministered in postexilic Judah, likely in the fifth century BC. The temple had been rebuilt, but renewed buildings had not produced renewed hearts.[[newline]][[newline]]Malachi confronted priests who offered animals that were blind, lame, or sick. His question exposed the issue: would a governor accept such a gift? (Malachi 1:8). The problem was not that they had stopped worshiping[[semicolon]] it was that they had brought God what cost them least.[[newline]][[newline]]The people seemed genuinely surprised by the prophet’s charges. They asked how they had wearied God and how they had robbed him. That blindness is part of Malachi’s warning. Once compromise becomes normal, people can confuse religious activity with faithfulness.[[newline]][[newline]]Malachi called Judah to return with whole hearts: honesty in relationships, justice toward workers and the vulnerable, covenant faithfulness, and reverent worship. His teaching about the tithe (Malachi 3:8–10) was not a technique for prosperity. It pressed a deeper question: can God be trusted with what we hold most tightly?[[newline]][[newline]]He also pointed ahead. The Lord would come to his temple, preceded by a messenger who prepared the way (Malachi 3:1). The New Testament identifies John the Baptist in that prophetic stream.[[newline]][[newline]]Healthy leaders make room for the message that interrupts complacency. They examine not only whether the work is getting done, but whether love, integrity, and reverence still animate it.[[newline]][[newline]]Before asking what should change around us, Malachi invites us to ask what has grown careless within us.[[newline]][[newline]]This pressure can appear in a budget meeting, a medical conversation, or an apology that needs to be made. Telling the truth does not require cruelty. It asks us to be specific, own our part, and give the other person information they need to make a real choice.[[newline]][[newline]]One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.[[newline]][[newline]]I want my words to do more than protect my image. Honest speech can be calm, careful, and kind. It can also be the first step toward repair that silence could never provide.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-03-21T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 27, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1958, $q$When Rescue Comes Through Someone Else$q$, $q$when-rescue-comes-through-someone-else$q$, replace(replace($pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.[[newline]][[newline]]Othniel’s story begins the repeated cycle in Judges: rebellion, oppression, crying out, God’s rescue, and a season of peace.[[newline]][[newline]]Othniel, the son of Kenaz and related to Caleb, first appears as the man who captured Debir and married Caleb’s daughter Aksah (Joshua 15:16–17[[semicolon]] Judges 1:12–13). He was already a proven warrior before he became Israel’s first judge.[[newline]][[newline]]Judges 3 tells us that Israel forgot the Lord and served the Baals and Asherahs. God gave them over to Cushan-rishathaim, king of Aram-naharaim, a Mesopotamian region associated with parts of modern Syria and Iraq. For eight years Israel lived under oppression (Judges 3:7–8).[[newline]][[newline]]When Israel cried out, the Lord raised up Othniel. The text is concise but decisive: “The Spirit of the Lord came on him, so that he became Israel’s judge and went to war” (Judges 3:10). Othniel defeated the oppressor, and the land had peace for forty years.[[newline]][[newline]]The point is not that Israel finally found a stronger personality. Their deliverance began with the Lord’s mercy and was carried out through a leader empowered by the Spirit. Othniel’s earlier courage mattered, but the text directs attention to God’s action.[[newline]][[newline]]This first cycle is also a warning. Israel’s suffering was connected to spiritual drift[[semicolon]] rescue was not permission to return to the same patterns. Leaders serve people well when they help them name what has gone wrong, cry out honestly to God, and receive help without mistaking the helper for the Savior.[[newline]][[newline]]Othniel points beyond temporary relief to the need for a rescuer who can break the deeper cycle of sin. In Christ, God provides that ultimate deliverance. Until then, ordinary faithfulness and Spirit-empowered leadership remain gifts through which God brings help.[[newline]][[newline]]That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech. It may begin with one clear sentence, one practical offer, or one refusal to look away.[[newline]][[newline]]I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-03-14T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 28, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1954, $q$The Secret of Being Known$q$, $q$the-secret-of-being-known$q$, replace($pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.[[newline]][[newline]]The leaders who change the world aren't always the loudest voices in the room.[[newline]][[newline]]Sometimes they're the ones who stay close enough to learn what can't be taught.[[newline]][[newline]]John was a fisherman's kid who became part of history's most influential inner circle. He never called himself by name in his own writings—just "the disciple whom Jesus loved." Not bragging. Describing.[[newline]][[newline]]At the most important dinner in history, everyone else sat upright.[[newline]][[newline]]John leaned in.[[newline]][[newline]]Literally. His head rested on Jesus' chest (John 13:23). Not professional distance. Not strategic positioning. Physical closeness.[[newline]][[newline]]We talk about mentorship like it's a program. A curriculum. A series of meetings with agendas.[[newline]][[newline]]John understood something different.[[newline]][[newline]]Proximity beats curriculum.[[newline]][[newline]]When Jesus hung dying, he looked down and saw John standing there (John 19:26-27). Everyone else had scattered. John stayed close.[[newline]][[newline]]That's what closeness earns you. Not promotion. Responsibility.[[newline]][[newline]]Years later, exiled to a rocky island called Patmos, John had a vision that would become the book of Revelation (Revelation 1:9-20). Isolated from everything, he was still connected to what mattered.[[newline]][[newline]]Here's what we're learning together:[[newline]][[newline]]Identity isn't earned. It's received.[[newline]][[newline]]John never introduced himself as "the best disciple" or "the smartest follower." He identified himself by how he was loved.[[newline]][[newline]]When Peter wanted to know who would betray Jesus, he didn't ask directly. He asked John to ask (John 13:24). Because John was close enough to whisper.[[newline]][[newline]]On resurrection morning, John and Peter both ran to the empty tomb (John 20:1-10). John got there first. But he waited. He let Peter go in.[[newline]][[newline]]Confident people can wait.[[newline]][[newline]]John outlived all the other disciples. He had more time to reflect, more years to understand. And what did he write about most?[[newline]][[newline]]Love.[[newline]][[newline]]Not strategies. Not systems. Not five steps to anything.[[newline]][[newline]]Just love (John 21:20-24).[[newline]][[newline]]The leader who stays close learns what the distant observer never will.[[newline]][[newline]]Maybe the question isn't "How do I get ahead?"[[newline]][[newline]]Maybe it's "How do I stay close?"[[newline]][[newline]]That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech.[[newline]][[newline]]I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-03-07T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 29, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1946, $q$When Waiting Becomes the Work$q$, $q$when-waiting-becomes-the-work$q$, replace(replace($pt$Waiting can make a person feel forgotten. Days keep moving while the answer, change, or relief we hoped for remains out of reach. The danger is not only disappointment[[semicolon]] it is letting delay convince us that nothing faithful can happen now.[[newline]][[newline]]Waiting is not empty time when it is anchored in God’s promise. It forms the discernment needed for faithful action.[[newline]][[newline]]Luke describes Simeon as righteous and devout, waiting for Israel’s consolation. The Holy Spirit had revealed that he would see the Messiah before he died (Luke 2:25–26).[[newline]][[newline]]Simeon’s great moment began with an ordinary visit to the temple. Mary and Joseph came to fulfill the law after Jesus’ birth, bringing the offering available to families of modest means: two birds (Luke 2:22–24). There was no public announcement and no visible sign of power.[[newline]][[newline]]Yet Simeon recognized the child. He took Jesus in his arms and praised God: “My eyes have seen your salvation” (Luke 2:29–30). He saw not merely a private comfort but God’s light for the nations and glory for Israel.[[newline]][[newline]]The years of waiting had not made Simeon passive. They had made him attentive. He stayed close enough to God’s promise to notice its fulfillment in a form others could easily overlook.[[newline]][[newline]]Leaders often want a timetable, a clear result, and a visible role. Simeon offers another picture: keep showing up, keep praying, keep practicing obedience, and let God determine the moment and the scale of the assignment.[[newline]][[newline]]Faithful waiting does not guarantee that we will get everything we want. It does cultivate a heart able to receive what God gives. Simeon could release his life in peace because God had kept his word.[[newline]][[newline]]The question is not simply, “When will my opportunity come?” It is also, “What kind of person am I becoming while I wait?”[[newline]][[newline]]A long search for work, an unresolved illness, or a strained relationship can narrow our attention to what has not happened. We still may be able to keep one promise, encourage one person, ask for help, or notice one good thing without pretending the pain is gone.[[newline]][[newline]]One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.[[newline]][[newline]]Waiting is not proof that a life has stopped mattering. I want to stay honest about what hurts while remaining available to the good I can receive and give today.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-02-28T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 30, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1930, $q$The Traitor Who Changed Everything$q$, $q$the-traitor-who-changed-everything$q$, replace(replace($pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.[[newline]][[newline]]Rahab’s story does not celebrate deception or self-preservation. It shows a woman who recognized the Lord’s authority and staked her future on him.[[newline]][[newline]]Rahab lived in Jericho, a fortified Canaanite city near the Jordan River. Joshua 2 identifies her as a prostitute, placing her at the edge of respectable society and making God’s welcome especially striking.[[newline]][[newline]]When Israel’s spies entered Jericho, Rahab had already heard what the city had heard: the Lord had dried up the Red Sea and had defeated kings east of the Jordan (Joshua 2:9–10). Jericho’s leaders heard the reports and prepared to resist. Rahab drew a different conclusion: “The Lord your God is God in heaven above and on earth beneath” (Joshua 2:11).[[newline]][[newline]]Her faith did not remain private. She hid the spies and asked that her family be spared. The scarlet cord at her window marked the household that would be rescued when Jericho fell (Joshua 2:18–21[[semicolon]] 6:22–25).[[newline]][[newline]]Rahab’s courage should not be flattened into a generic lesson about disruption. She entrusted herself to the God of Israel, separated from a doomed allegiance, and acted to protect those in her care. Hebrews remembers her for faith[[semicolon]] James points to her actions as evidence that faith was real (Hebrews 11:31[[semicolon]] James 2:25).[[newline]][[newline]]God brought Rahab into Israel, and Matthew includes her in Jesus’ genealogy (Matthew 1:5). Her past did not limit God’s mercy or purpose.[[newline]][[newline]]Leaders need Rahab’s clarity: listen carefully, name reality truthfully, and act on allegiance to God even when the surrounding system insists that nothing must change.[[newline]][[newline]]That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech. It may begin with one clear sentence, one practical offer, or one refusal to look away.[[newline]][[newline]]One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.[[newline]][[newline]]I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-02-21T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 31, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1911, $q$The Power of Stepping Back$q$, $q$the-power-of-stepping-back$q$, replace($pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.[[newline]][[newline]]The hardest leadership move isn't climbing higher. It's knowing when to make room for someone else. Real influence often grows by shrinking.[[newline]][[newline]]John the Baptist was a first-century leader who built one of the largest movements of his time. Crowds traveled miles to hear him speak. He had power, platform, and momentum. Then he gave it all away on purpose.[[newline]][[newline]]He had thousands of followers.[[newline]][[newline]]Political leaders feared him. Religious elites watched his every move. People came from everywhere just to be near him (Matthew 3:5-6).[[newline]][[newline]]Then one day, he pointed to someone else and said, "Follow him instead."[[newline]][[newline]]Wait. What?[[newline]][[newline]]Most leaders spend their whole lives building. Gathering. Growing. We measure success by how many people listen to us. How much influence we hold.[[newline]][[newline]]John did the opposite.[[newline]][[newline]]"He must increase, but I must decrease" (John 3:30).[[newline]][[newline]]This wasn't failure. This was strategy.[[newline]][[newline]]John understood something we easily miss: sometimes the best thing you can do is get smaller.[[newline]][[newline]]His team noticed. They came to him worried. "Everyone is going to him now" (John 3:26). They expected John to fight back. To compete. To protect his brand.[[newline]][[newline]]Instead, John smiled.[[newline]][[newline]]"A person can receive only what is given them from heaven" (John 3:27).[[newline]][[newline]]Here's what we're learning together:[[newline]][[newline]]The voice in the wilderness knew his job was to prepare the way (John 1:23). Not to be the way.[[newline]][[newline]]The best leaders know the difference.[[newline]][[newline]]They build things bigger than themselves. They prepare paths for others to walk. They find joy when someone else gets the spotlight.[[newline]][[newline]]John never wrote a book. Never built an empire. He was executed in a prison cell (Matthew 14:10).[[newline]][[newline]]But Jesus called him the greatest person ever born (Matthew 11:11).[[newline]][[newline]]Maybe greatness isn't about how big we become.[[newline]][[newline]]Maybe it's about making room for something greater.[[newline]][[newline]]That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech. It may begin with one clear sentence, one practical offer, or one refusal to look away.[[newline]][[newline]]I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-02-14T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 32, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1928, $q$Who Stays When Everyone Leaves$q$, $q$who-stays-when-everyone-leaves$q$, replace(replace($pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.[[newline]][[newline]]Mary Magdalene is remembered not for a title but for faithful presence at the moments when following Jesus was dangerous and costly.[[newline]][[newline]]Luke names Mary Magdalene among the women healed by Jesus and among those who supported his ministry from their resources (Luke 8:1–3). Scripture does not identify her with the unnamed sinful woman in Luke 7[[semicolon]] her story deserves to stand on its own testimony.[[newline]][[newline]]At the crucifixion, many disciples had fled. The Gospels place Mary Magdalene among the women who remained near Jesus, witnessing his death and burial (John 19:25[[semicolon]] Mark 15:40–47). She did not solve the crisis. She stayed close to the suffering of her Lord.[[newline]][[newline]]Then, before daylight on the first day of the week, Mary went to the tomb. John records her grief, confusion, and perseverance. When the risen Jesus called her by name, she recognized him. He sent her to tell the disciples that he was ascending to his Father and their Father (John 20:11–18).[[newline]][[newline]]Mary was not elevated because she outperformed the apostles. She was entrusted with the first resurrection announcement because she was present, attentive, and ready to obey. Her witness is part of the Gospel’s own account, not an embellishment.[[newline]][[newline]]In leadership, presence is often undervalued because it cannot be measured easily. Yet people remember who came near in grief, who remained when a project failed, and who kept showing up when there was no applause.[[newline]][[newline]]Mary Magdalene reminds us that courage is not always a speech or a strategy. Sometimes it is returning at dawn, grieving honestly, and listening for the voice of Jesus.[[newline]][[newline]]That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech. It may begin with one clear sentence, one practical offer, or one refusal to look away.[[newline]][[newline]]I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-02-07T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 33, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1926, $q$The Power of Letting Go$q$, $q$the-power-of-letting-go$q$, replace(replace($pt$Courage is easiest to admire after the danger has passed. In the moment, a faithful choice can look costly, lonely, and uncertain. We may know what should be done and still feel every reason to wait for someone else.[[newline]][[newline]]Mary’s “yes” to God was not passive resignation. It was courageous trust amid risk, uncertainty, and a future she could not manage.[[newline]][[newline]]Mary was a young Jewish woman from Nazareth, pledged to Joseph. Luke presents her as a recipient of grace, not as someone whose social status prepared her for public importance (Luke 1:26–38).[[newline]][[newline]]Gabriel’s announcement unsettled every expectation Mary had for her life. She would bear a son by the Holy Spirit, and that son would reign forever. Mary asked how this could be[[semicolon]] she did not pretend to understand. Then she answered, “I am the Lord’s servant. May it be to me according to your word” (Luke 1:34–38).[[newline]][[newline]]That response did not remove the cost. It placed Mary’s reputation, relationships, and safety in God’s hands. Her song in Luke 1:46–55 shows that surrender is not weakness. She praised the God who lifts the humble, keeps his covenant, and overturns human pride.[[newline]][[newline]]Mary continued to live with mystery. She pondered the words and events surrounding Jesus’ birth (Luke 2:19, 51). At Cana she directed servants to do whatever Jesus said (John 2:5). At the cross she stood near her dying son (John 19:25–27). In each scene she neither controls Jesus nor withdraws from him.[[newline]][[newline]]Leaders often equate strength with having a plan and securing an outcome. Mary models a truer strength: discern what God is doing, obey what he asks, and remain present when the next step is painful or unclear.[[newline]][[newline]]Surrender is not indifference. It is active trust in the God whose purposes are wiser than ours.[[newline]][[newline]]That moment may come when a coworker is treated unfairly, a vulnerable person needs help, or a decision is being hidden behind polite language. Courage does not require a dramatic speech. It may begin with one clear sentence, one practical offer, or one refusal to look away.[[newline]][[newline]]One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.[[newline]][[newline]]I may not control the result, but I can decide whether fear will make the choice for me. The next faithful step is often small enough to take and important enough not to postpone.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-01-31T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 34, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1909, $q$When Your Best Move Becomes Your Worst$q$, $q$when-your-best-move-becomes-your-worst$q$, replace(replace($pt$Harm often begins before anyone sees the damage. A private resentment, a protected lie, or a decision to use another person can grow until someone else pays the price. These stories matter because our choices never stay as private as we imagine.[[newline]][[newline]]Asa’s reign warns that success can quietly replace the humility that made faithfulness possible in the first place.[[newline]][[newline]]Asa ruled Judah for forty-one years. Early in his reign he removed idols, sought the Lord, and led reforms that brought peace (2 Chronicles 14–15).[[newline]][[newline]]When Judah faced a vast Cushite army, Asa prayed with clarity: “Help us, Lord our God, for we rely on you” (2 Chronicles 14:11). God gave Judah victory. Asa’s strength was not military superiority but dependence on the Lord.[[newline]][[newline]]Later, when Baasha of Israel threatened Judah, Asa chose a different path. He sent treasures from the temple and palace to Ben-hadad of Aram, asking him to break his treaty with Israel (2 Chronicles 16:1–6). The immediate problem eased, but the prophet Hanani confronted Asa for relying on a foreign king rather than on God.[[newline]][[newline]]Hanani’s rebuke reached deeper than one political decision. “The eyes of the Lord range throughout the earth to strengthen those whose hearts are fully committed to him” (2 Chronicles 16:9). Asa responded defensively, imprisoning the prophet and oppressing some of the people.[[newline]][[newline]]The contrast is sobering. The leader who sought God in a crisis later trusted resources, alliances, and control. Scripture does not make his illness a simple object lesson about medical care[[semicolon]] it notes that in his disease he did not seek the Lord but only physicians (2 Chronicles 16:12).[[newline]][[newline]]Asa’s story invites leaders to ask: has competence replaced prayer? Has success made correction feel threatening? The practices that sustained a faithful beginning—humility, counsel, dependence, repentance—remain necessary to finish well.[[newline]][[newline]]A smaller version can happen at work or at home. We can hide a mistake, shift blame, exclude someone, or use sharp words to protect our standing. The immediate problem may move away from us, but it lands on a person who deserves honesty and care.[[newline]][[newline]]One useful pause is to ask what I am trying to protect and who may bear the cost. That question can expose a goal that needs to change before the decision is made.[[newline]][[newline]]Before I protect myself at someone else's expense, I want to stop and name the likely harm. Truth may cost me something now, but passing that cost to another person is not faithfulness.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-01-24T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 35, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1950, $q$The Person Who Believed First$q$, $q$the-person-who-believed-first$q$, replace($pt$A kind word can help someone keep going. Sometimes, though, they also need an introduction. They need someone to explain their work, answer a concern, or help them find a place to serve. Encouragement becomes useful when it helps a person take the next step.[[newline]][[newline]]Barnabas (BAHR-nuh-buhs) was a Jewish follower of Jesus from the island of Cyprus. His given name was Joseph. The apostles called him “son of encouragement” (Acts 4:36, NIV). He had already sold a field and given the money to help people in need.[[newline]][[newline]]Then he helped a man whom believers had good reason to fear.[[newline]][[newline]]Saul, later known as Paul, had threatened Jesus' followers and sought to arrest them. After encountering Jesus, he began preaching the faith he had attacked. But when he came to Jerusalem, the believers there did not trust that he had changed (Acts 9:1–28).[[newline]][[newline]]This was the same Jerusalem whose ancient center lies within and around today's Old City. Our map page locates that area. Acts does not identify the house where this meeting happened.[[newline]][[newline]]Barnabas brought Saul to the apostles, the church's leading witnesses to Jesus. He explained Saul's encounter with Jesus and his bold preaching afterward. Then Saul was able to stay with the believers and speak openly about his faith (Acts 9:27–28).[[newline]][[newline]]Barnabas gave them something to consider. He did not simply demand that they stop being afraid. He shared what had happened and brought the people together.[[newline]][[newline]]Later, when another church needed teaching, Barnabas went to find Saul and brought him into the work. They taught together for a year (Acts 11:25–26). His encouragement included both an introduction and a lasting partnership.[[newline]][[newline]]I notice how practical this is. It is easy to admire someone's ability from a distance. Helping them use it asks more of me. I may need to make a call, explain what I have seen, or spend time beside them.[[newline]][[newline]]That does not mean ignoring harm or promising that another person will never fail. Trust needs truth. An honest introduction can include limits as well as strengths.[[newline]][[newline]]Perhaps someone you know has done careful work that others have not seen. With their permission, share a specific example with someone who needs that help. Or invite them to contribute to a task you can do together.[[newline]][[newline]]Encouragement does not have to predict a great future. It can help someone take one real step today.$pt$, '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-01-17T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 36, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1899, $q$The Kid They Almost Passed Over$q$, $q$the-kid-they-almost-passed-over$q$, replace($pt$BIG IDEA[[newline]][[newline]]Sometimes the person least likely to lead is exactly who we need.[[newline]][[newline]]The nervous kid. The one who blushes. The stomach-ache before every meeting.[[newline]][[newline]]What if that's not a bug? What if it's a feature?[[newline]][[newline]]BACKGROUND[[newline]][[newline]]Timothy was a young man from a small town in ancient Turkey. His mentor, Paul, saw something in him that Timothy couldn't see himself. He went from a timid kid with a queasy stomach to leading one of the most influential movements in history.[[newline]][[newline]]STORY[[newline]][[newline]]He was crying again.[[newline]][[newline]]We know this because Paul mentions Timothy's tears in his final letter (2 Timothy 1:4). This wasn't a one-time thing. Timothy was emotional. Sensitive. The ancient world had a word for people like him.[[newline]][[newline]]Weak.[[newline]][[newline]]Yet Paul picked him anyway (Acts 16:1-3).[[newline]][[newline]]Here's what we often miss. Paul didn't choose Timothy despite his temperament. He chose him because of it.[[newline]][[newline]]Timothy had frequent stomach problems. Paul told him to drink a little wine for his ailments (1 Timothy 5:23). This wasn't casual advice. It was medicine for a man whose anxiety lived in his gut.[[newline]][[newline]]Sound familiar?[[newline]][[newline]]The thing is, nervous people notice things. They prepare more. They care more about getting it right.[[newline]][[newline]]Paul saw this.[[newline]][[newline]]"Don't let anyone look down on you because you are young," he wrote (1 Timothy 4:12). Notice what he didn't say. He didn't say, "Stop being nervous." He didn't say, "Toughen up."[[newline]][[newline]]He said, "Lead anyway."[[newline]][[newline]]Timothy's fear wasn't the enemy. His fear kept him humble. It kept him dependent. It kept him teachable.[[newline]][[newline]]The real enemy was letting others define what leadership looks like.[[newline]][[newline]]We've all been passed over. Too young. Too quiet. Too anxious. Too different.[[newline]][[newline]]But here's what Timothy teaches us: the qualities that make us feel disqualified might be the very things that make us effective.[[newline]][[newline]]Paul didn't need a tough guy. He needed a faithful one. Someone who would show up even when his hands were shaking. Someone who would speak even when his voice trembled.[[newline]][[newline]]"For God gave us a spirit not of fear," Paul reminded him, "but of power and love and self-control" (2 Timothy 1:7).[[newline]][[newline]]Timothy became one of the early church's most trusted leaders. Not because he conquered his anxiety. But because he refused to let it have the final word.[[newline]][[newline]]Maybe leadership isn't about feeling confident.[[newline]][[newline]]Maybe it's about showing up anyway.$pt$, '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-01-10T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 37, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1890, $q$Your Side Hustle Is Your Superpower$q$, $q$your-side-hustle-is-your-superpower$q$, replace($pt$BIG IDEA[[newline]][[newline]]The most influential leaders often aren't building platforms. They're building tents. Their day job becomes the doorway to something greater.[[newline]][[newline]]BACKGROUND[[newline]][[newline]]Priscilla and Aquila were first-century tentmakers. They ran a small business in Corinth. But their workshop became a training ground that shaped the early church.[[newline]][[newline]]STORY[[newline]][[newline]]A refugee couple opens a tent shop in a foreign city.[[newline]][[newline]]Then the most famous leader of their generation walks in. Needs a job.[[newline]][[newline]]They hire him.[[newline]][[newline]]That's how Paul met Priscilla and Aquila (Acts 18:1-3). Not in a temple. Not at a conference. In a leather shop, stitching fabric.[[newline]][[newline]]Here's what we tend to miss.[[newline]][[newline]]This couple didn't separate their work from their purpose. They fused them. Their business was the vehicle. Relationship was the fuel.[[newline]][[newline]]Later, a brilliant speaker named Apollos came to town. He was eloquent and passionate. But he had gaps in his knowledge.[[newline]][[newline]]Did they correct him publicly? Call him out? Post about it?[[newline]][[newline]]No.[[newline]][[newline]]They invited him to dinner (Acts 18:26).[[newline]][[newline]]Private correction. Public support. That's how you develop people without destroying them.[[newline]][[newline]]Paul later wrote that they "risked their own necks" for him (Romans 16:3-4). He called them "co-workers." Not followers. Partners.[[newline]][[newline]]And their home? It became a church (1 Corinthians 16:19).[[newline]][[newline]]Not a building with a budget. A living room with an open door.[[newline]][[newline]]We often think influence requires a stage. A title. A platform.[[newline]][[newline]]Priscilla and Aquila show us something different.[[newline]][[newline]]Influence grows through proximity. Through shared meals and honest conversations. Through showing up, day after day, with your door open.[[newline]][[newline]]They weren't waiting for permission to lead. They were already leading—with thread and needle, with food and hospitality, with quiet courage.[[newline]][[newline]]Maybe you don't need a bigger platform.[[newline]][[newline]]Maybe you need a longer table.$pt$, '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2027-01-03T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 38, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1884, $q$When Courage Looks Like Surrender$q$, $q$when-courage-looks-like-surrender$q$, replace($pt$BIG IDEA[[newline]][[newline]]The strongest leaders aren't those who fight back the hardest.[[newline]][[newline]]They're the ones who know when to put down the sword.[[newline]][[newline]]Sometimes the most powerful thing we can do is refuse to defend ourselves.[[newline]][[newline]]BACKGROUND[[newline]][[newline]]Stephen was one of the first leaders chosen to serve the early church. He handled the daily distribution of food to widows (Acts 6:1-6). His job was logistics and care. Nothing flashy. But his life ended in a way nobody expected.[[newline]][[newline]]STORY[[newline]][[newline]]They said his face looked like an angel's.[[newline]][[newline]]Right there in the courtroom. With angry men shouting lies about him. With his life on the line. Stephen's face was calm. Radiant. At peace (Acts 6:15).[[newline]][[newline]]Think about that for a second.[[newline]][[newline]]When we're falsely accused, what happens to our face? Our body? Our voice?[[newline]][[newline]]We tense up. We prepare to fight back. We build our case.[[newline]][[newline]]Stephen did the opposite.[[newline]][[newline]]He stood before the most powerful men in his world. He told them the truth. And when they grabbed stones to kill him, he didn't run. He didn't beg. He didn't curse.[[newline]][[newline]]He looked up and said, "Lord, don't hold this against them" (Acts 7:60).[[newline]][[newline]]Sound familiar?[[newline]][[newline]]Jesus said the same words from the cross (Luke 23:34).[[newline]][[newline]]Stephen became the first person to die the way Jesus died. Not just physically. But spiritually. Forgiving his killers with his final breath.[[newline]][[newline]]Here's what we miss about this moment.[[newline]][[newline]]Stephen was smart enough to defend himself. He'd just delivered a brilliant speech about Israel's history (Acts 7:1-53). He knew the law. He knew the arguments.[[newline]][[newline]]He chose not to use them.[[newline]][[newline]]Because there's a moment when defending yourself stops being brave.[[newline]][[newline]]And surrender becomes the real courage.[[newline]][[newline]]In the crowd that day stood a young man named Saul. He watched everything (Acts 7:58). He approved of the killing.[[newline]][[newline]]Years later, that same Saul—now Paul—would write about a different kind of power. The kind that shows up in weakness (2 Corinthians 12:9).[[newline]][[newline]]Maybe he learned it from watching Stephen die.[[newline]][[newline]]We're told to stand our ground. Defend our reputation. Fight for what's ours.[[newline]][[newline]]But some victories can't be won by fighting.[[newline]][[newline]]Some can only be won by letting go.$pt$, '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2026-12-27T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 39, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2772, $q$Loyalty Needs an Honest Question$q$, $q$loyalty-needs-an-honest-question$q$, replace($pt$The people we support can hurt others with the help we give them. That is why loyalty needs room for an honest question. Before defending a friend or backing a leader, we can ask what our support is making possible.[[newline]][[newline]]Abimelech (uh-BIM-uh-lek) was a son of Gideon (GID-ee-uhn), an Israelite leader who had fought to free his people from an enemy army. After his father's death, Abimelech wanted power. He turned to his mother's relatives in Shechem (SHEE-kehm), a city in the hill country. In the NIV Bible, his name is spelled Abimelek.[[newline]][[newline]]Ancient Shechem stood near today's Nablus (NAH-blus), in the West Bank. The map page marks its ruins about a mile east-southeast of the modern city's center.[[newline]][[newline]]Abimelech asked the citizens to choose him rather than his many brothers. His family connection helped win them over. Their reason was simple: “He is related to us” (Judges 9:3, NIV).[[newline]][[newline]]They gave him silver. He used it to hire followers, then murdered his brothers. One younger brother escaped. After the killings, the city's people crowned Abimelech king (Judges 9:4–6).[[newline]][[newline]]The story does not leave responsibility with him alone. It also names the people of Shechem who helped him commit the murders (Judges 9:24). Their support had consequences beyond their own circle.[[newline]][[newline]]After three years, the alliance broke apart. Abimelech attacked the city that had backed him. More people died. His own violent rule ended during another attack, when a woman defending a tower struck him with a stone (Judges 9:22–54).[[newline]][[newline]]I find the early choice more unsettling than the final battle. Those citizens had a reason to feel close to him. But a family tie could not tell them whether his actions deserved their support.[[newline]][[newline]]Our disagreements are usually far less extreme. Still, that distinction matters. Knowing someone well does not settle whether a complaint about them is true. Sharing their goals does not excuse the way they treat another person.[[newline]][[newline]]We can care about someone and ask them to answer for a specific action. We can listen to the person who was hurt before preparing a defense. We can stop repeating a claim when we cannot support it.[[newline]][[newline]]The next time loyalty pulls you toward a quick defense, pause long enough to ask: What happened, and who is paying the cost? Caring about our people can include helping them face the truth.$pt$, '[[newline]]', chr(10)), $q$2026-12-20T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 40, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$) on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
